{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 22, :comments ["<b>And Moses returned… why…</b> The intention of the verse is along these lines: <b>why have You done evil</b> — meaning, for You told me nothing except that He would harden his heart and not send the people, but not that He would make the suffering heavy upon them. And if you would say that the appointed time had not yet arrived to lighten the bondage from upon them, and this is the cause of the evil — if so, <b>why is it that You have sent me</b>, meaning, at this time; and You should have waited until the appointed end of the bondage." "<b>Further,</b> it intends according to what we wrote above, that HaShem revealed to him that even though the time of redemption had not yet drawn near, nevertheless He was bringing it about in advance to save them from the affliction, and when the time of redemption would arrive after twelve months He would bring them up. And this is what he said: <b>why have You done evil to this people</b>. And if you would say that it is better that they endure this trouble for the sake of the redemption — if so, ״לָמָּה זֶה שְׁלַחְתָּנִי״ — for after all, the entire essence of the mission before the time of redemption was for the purpose of removing the bondage, and if there is no beneficial purpose to the matter except the exodus and not saving them from trouble, if so <b>why is it that You have sent me.</b> And he hinted in His saying <b>זֶה</b> at the twelve months that Israel remained delayed in Egypt from this time until the redemption, and Moses feared lest this trouble that Pharaoh had made heavy upon Israel would continue thus until the time of their exodus from Egypt." "<b>Further,</b> it may intend: since among Israel there was a group that was enslaved and a group that was not enslaved — and this was the tribe of Levi — therefore he said concerning the group that was enslaved: <b>why have You done evil to this people</b>; he said ״הַזֶּה״ to exclude those who were not enslaved. And concerning the group to whom no evil had been caused, he said: <b>why is it that You have sent me</b> — meaning, I achieved nothing at all in my mission." "<b>Further,</b> it may intend along this line: <b>why have You done evil</b> to Your people, and necessarily, through the very act of the mission the evil came about; if so, <b>why is it that You have sent me</b>. And he hinted in the word ״זֶה״ to say that it was on account of this that he had been reluctant to go at the time of the mission."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 5, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:24:55.790365374Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}